linux-t2-mbp161 packaging: Linux 7.2.15 + t2linux e167207 (KVER=7.2.10), then yuters e77b03f 0001–0005, then extra-01..13 (KaiT2en 8de9546, klizas 5181c93). PATCHES.md says where each patch comes from, what it does, and how it overlaps with t2linux and Omarchy 4.5.

First install: `mbp16-1 apply kernel --build` (the snapshot vendored in this directory), or `mbp16-1 apply kernel --pkg` with the CI packages in prebuilt/. Those are refused unless they match prebuilt.sha256 and this directory still hashes to prebuilt.inputs (README.md D4, Prebuilt packages). This snapshot is bumped by hand past linux-t2-arch, whose `main` (7.2.14) pins t2linux patches for 7.3.

When stock linux-t2 moves: `omarchy update` (not `pacman -Syu`) upgrades recovery only, then `mbp16-1-limine-quiet` (Limine rewrites `/boot/limine.conf`). Daily does not follow until `mbp16-1 update kernel`. That command never moves this directory to an older kernel: while linux-t2-arch is older, it rebuilds the vendored tree as is (lib/kernel_sync.py exit 3). Once linux-t2-arch is newer and consistent, it rebases pkgver/pkgrel/T2_PATCH_HASH/checksums/config.x86_64 from https://github.com/NoaHimesaka1873/linux-t2-arch. Either way it keeps the 18 local patches, leaves CONFIG_RUST off, and does not provide/replace linux. After makepkg it re-runs `mbp16-1-limine-quiet`.

Do not use yuters main or install.sh. Do not install KaiT2en's DKMS or runtime-pm packages on top of this kernel (see PATCHES.md). If a yuters-000*.patch or extra-*.patch rejects on a new t2linux base, it needs a refresh. Stop.
